WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.730
Last month I asked a head teacher how

2
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.190
many AI tools her school uses.

3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:07.930
She said one.

4
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.180
We counted together and found seven,

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.640
from a marking helper to a WhatsApp

6
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.340
bot that answers parents.

7
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.680
You can't protect what you haven't listed.

8
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.770
Today: a threat model for your

9
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.170
school's AI, the risks it shows,

10
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.130
and which to fix first.

11
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.910
A threat model starts with an inventory.

12
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:34.530
For every AI tool, write four things:

13
00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.090
what data it touches, what it can do, who

14
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.230
uses it, and who looks after it.

15
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.950
A chatbot that only reads lesson

16
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.030
notes is very different from one

17
00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.010
that can message parents.

18
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.360
Next, find the paths in.

19
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.510
Anything the AI reads that you didn't

20
00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.290
write is untrusted: a web page, a pupil's

21
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.210
essay, a parent's message, a tool's

22
00:00:59.210 --> 00:00:59.490
result.

23
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.020
Each one can carry a hidden

24
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.560
order, or simply a mistake.

25
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.930
Then score each risk.

26
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.140
Likelihood from one to three:

27
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.200
how likely is it to happen?

28
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.620
Impact from one to three:

29
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.940
how bad if it does?

30
00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.630
Multiply them: a nine needs action now,

31
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.970
a two can wait and be watched.

32
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.250
I keep our school's risk register

33
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.890
as a short list in Python.

34
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.940
For each tool, it multiplies

35
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.500
likelihood by impact.

36
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.910
Anything scoring six or more is

37
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.650
printed, so it gets an owner.

38
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:40.340
Quick check!

39
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.470
The lesson-plan helper scores two.

40
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.130
The parents' chatbot, which can

41
00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.190
send messages, scores nine.

42
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.100
Which do we fix first?

43
00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.710
The parents' chatbot.

44
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.870
It scores highest, and it can act: a

45
00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.170
hidden order in a parent's message could

46
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.390
make it send something to every family.

47
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.950
Start at the top of the list, and

48
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.270
look hardest at tools that act.

49
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.750
For each top risk, choose a

50
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.530
control and name an owner.

51
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.020
Keep parents' messages as

52
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.580
data, not orders.

53
00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.650
Let the chatbot draft, and a person send.

54
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.500
And give one named teacher the job

55
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.940
of checking it each term.

56
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.340
In the app, hide an order in three places:

57
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.060
a note, a book title and a village name.

58
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.840
Run each one and record what happens:

59
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.680
ignored, slipped through or sent.

60
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.760
Then switch on each defence and

61
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.980
see which path it closes.

62
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.140
Your turn.

63
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.720
Our marking helper scores six, and

64
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.240
everyone says somebody should check it.

65
00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.510
Who owns the risk?

66
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.290
One named person, with a date.

67
00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.540
Remember: list every tool, find the paths

68
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.140
in, score likelihood times impact, and

69
00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.660
give the top risks a control and an owner.

70
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.340
Try it at school this week: with two

71
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.740
colleagues, or your pupils' AI club,

72
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.220
list every AI tool you use.

73
00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.230
Score each risk on the board, and

74
00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.990
agree an owner for the top three.

75
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.940
Then share the list with

76
00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:22.580
your head teacher.
